  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA vertically stacked/closed low slowly tracking east across \ncentral IL serves as the primary lifting mechanism for showers \nand isolated thunderstorms into tonight. Latest analysis \ndepicts a moist airmass anchored beneath the low, with \nprecipitable water (PWAT) values around 1.8 inches. The nose of\nmodest instability (surface-based CAPE near 1000 J/kg) extends \nas far west as the IL/IN border. Within the zone of maximized \nascent just north and east of the mid level low center over east\ncentral IL, localized rainfall rates up to 2 inches per hour \nare possible through early evening. A Flash Flood Watch remains \nin effect for east central IL through 06z, where localized \ntotals of 2-5 inches have been seen in recent HRRR guidance.\n\nBeneath the low and along the trailing surface trough axis in \neast- central Illinois, RAP and HRRR boundary-layer profiles \nshow enhanced low-level vorticity, steep 0-3 km lapse rates \nwhere cloud breaks have developed, and minimal deep-layer \nshear. The Non-Supercell Tornado (NST) parameter highlights \nvalues of 1 to 2 across this area through 23z. These conditions\nfavor brief, shear-zone funnel clouds - see latest Special\nWeather Statement (SPS) for more info. \n\nRainfall diminishes from the west tonight as the closed shifts \neastward. Behind the low, MSLP gradient tightens, and will\npromote 20-30 mph wind gusts from the north-northeast through\nthis evening.  Post- frontal low stratus and patchy fog are \nlikely to persist through daybreak Sunday. CAMs show isolated\ninstability showers developing over eastern IL Sunday afternoon,\nand have 20-30% PoPs to cover this.\n\nEnsemble and deterministic model suites are in strong \nagreement regarding transient upper-level ridging across the \nMississippi Valley Mon-Tue. Dry weather prevails for this period\nwith NBM guidance indicating seasonable high temperatures in \nthe low-mid 80s.\n\nForecast guidance continues to show a mid-latitude shortwave \ntrough digging out of the Northern Plains for the last half of \nthe week. Notable model disagreements exist regarding the track \nand intensity of this feature, though the consensus show a front\nslowly moving in from the north, then possibly stalling.\nResulting chance PoPs from the NBM extend from Wed-Fri.\nGenerally weak shear should limit organized severe potential,\nwith many ML/AI severe outlook products highlighting around a 5%\nrisk for much the Fri-Sat periods.",
